"text": "This is one of the most iconic photos of all time. Einstein, Bohr, Schrodinger, Heisenberg, 17 Nobel Prize winners in one frame. They were gathered in Brussels for a week-long meeting that would rewrite physics. And at that meeting, Einstein started an argument he was certain he'd win, but he didn't. And the idea that stuck is arguably the reason we even have AI today. It came down to one question. Is the universe certain, or is it just odds? Einstein's worldview was built on certainty. He believed that if you knew everything about a system, you could predict what it would do next. That's what he meant by his famous line, God does not play dice. But a younger physicist named Niels Bohr landed on the opposite conclusion. Down at the level of atoms, Bohr said, there is no exact outcome waiting to be found. There are only probabilities. You can know the odds of what happens next and nothing more. Einstein found this almost offensive, so he went to war. Every morning that week, he'd show up with a new thought experiment, some clever trap built to prove Bohr wrong. And every night, Bohr would stay up tearing the logic apart until he had his answer ready. One by one, Einstein's traps fell. The most celebrated physicist of the century couldn't land a convincing blow to Bohr's logic, and the probability hypothesis won out. Probability is what enabled physicists to understand the atom, And understanding the atom is what gave us the semiconductor, the chip, and the hardware AI runs on. But it goes deeper than the hardware. When ChatGPT writes you a sentence, it isn't reaching for the correct word, it's reaching for the most probable one again and again. Einstein said God doesn't play dice. A hundred years later, we built machines that do. And he wasn't the only genius who bet against the idea that won. We pulled together the stories of 13 of the most successful companies in technology and what they ALMOST got wrong. Comment book and we'll send it over.",
